#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166440 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c81626a0-6613-5f5d-9dda-df853958c571" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution of a Laplace Transformed Initial Value Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "d9715a0c-6808-5db8-b0b4-5bedc4f2e9e8" :question/math-academy-id "e-21155" :question/problem "${x_{1}^{′}(t) =-3x_{1}(t) + x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = 3x_{1}(t) - x_{2}(t)$,\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $\\mathcal{L}$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables $X_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\!\\left\\{x_1(t) \\right\\}$ and $X_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\!\\left\\{x_2(t) \\right\\}$.\nFind the solution of the new system in terms of the parameter $s$." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n{L{x_{1}^{′}(t)} = L{-3x_{1}(t) + x_{2}(t)}; L{x_{2}^{′}(t)} = L{3x_{1}(t) - x_{2}(t)}\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n{sL{x_{1}(t)} - x_{1}(0) =-3L{x_{1}(t)} + L{x_{2}(t)}; sL{x_{2}(t)} - x_{2}(0) = 3L{x_{1}(t)} - L{x_{2}(t)}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to the following system:\n\n$$\n{sX_{1}(s) - 4 =-3X_{1}(s) + X_{2}(s); sX_{2}(s) - 4 = 3X_{1}(s) - X_{2}(s)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nX_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\!\\left\\{x_1(t) \\right\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nX_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\!\\left\\{x_2(t) \\right\\}\n$$\n\nRewriting the system in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{(s + 3)X_{1}(s) - X_{2}(s) = 4; - 3X_{1}(s) + (s + 1)X_{2}(s) = 4\n$$\n\nNow, from the first equation, we have\n\n$$\nX_2(s) = (s+3)X_1(s) - 4\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3X_{1}(s) + (s + 1)[(s + 3)X_{1}(s) - 4] &= 4 \\\\\n- 3X_{1}(s) + (s + 1)(s + 3)X_{1}(s) - 4(s + 1) &= 4 \\\\\n[(s + 1)(s + 3) - 3]X_{1}(s) &= 4 + 4(s + 1) \\\\\ns(s + 4)X_{1}(s) &= 4s + 8 \\\\\n&= X_{1}(s) = (4(s + 2))/(s(s + 4))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find $X_2(s)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX_{2}(s) &= (s + 3)X_{1}(s) - 4 \\\\\n&= (s + 3)((4(s + 2))/(s(s + 4))) - 4 \\\\\n&= (4(s + 3)(s + 2) - 4s(s + 4))/(s(s + 4)) \\\\\n&= (4(s^{2} + 5s + 6) - 4(s^{2} + 4s))/(s(s + 4)) \\\\\n&= (4s^{2} + 20s + 24 - 4s^{2} - 16s)/(s(s + 4)) \\\\\n&= (4(s + 6))/(s(s + 4))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nX_1(s) =\\dfrac{4(s+2)}{s(s+4)}, \\qquad X_2(s) = \\dfrac{4(s+6)}{s(s+4)}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166441 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb88b85b-efa1-56db-8655-4b49703dda6a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Given the Laplace Transformed Solution"}] :question/id #uuid "ec8e6fde-504e-581f-b465-6a9bc3927d77" :question/math-academy-id "e-21156" :question/problem "${x_{1}^{′}(t) = 5x_{1}(t) - x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) =-5x_{1}(t) + x_{2}(t)$,\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution\n$X_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{x_1(t) \\right\\} = \\dfrac{4}{s} + \\dfrac{1}{s-6}, \\qquad X_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{x_2(t) \\right\\} = \\dfrac{20}{s} - \\dfrac{1}{s-6}$.\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem." :question/worked-solution "To find $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t)$ satisfying the original initial value problem, we apply the inverse Laplace transform to $X_1(s)$ and $X_2(s){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}(t) &= L^{-1}{X_{1}(s)} \\\\\n&= L^{-1}{\\frac{4}{s} + \\frac{1}{s - 6}} \\\\\n&= 4L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}} + L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s - 6}} \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 1 + e^{6 \\cdot t} \\\\\n&= 4 + e^{6t} \\\\\nx_{2}(t) &= L^{-1}{X_{2}(s)} \\\\\n&= L^{-1}{\\frac{20}{s} - \\frac{1}{s - 6}} \\\\\n&= 20L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}} - L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s - 6}} \\\\\n&= 20 \\cdot 1 - e^{6 \\cdot t} \\\\\n&= 20 - e^{6t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}(t) \\\\ x_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}4 + e^{6t} \\\\ 20 - e^{6t}\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}e^{6t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166442 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd7b8fc8-26d3-5551-9a46-45725bd863a6" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Laplace Transform to a System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "0b2a74d8-1ce2-5055-a843-1456b1878cf7" :question/math-academy-id "e-21157" :question/problem "${x_{1}^{′}(t) =-3x_{1}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = 9x_{1}(t) + 5x_{2}(t)$,\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n${[math]\\phantom{XXX}[/math] =-3X_{1}(s); [math]\\phantom{XXX}[/math] = [math]\\phantom{XXXXXXXX}[/math]$\nwhere $X_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{x_1(t) \\right\\}$ and $X_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{x_2(t) \\right\\}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t)$, respectively." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n{L{x_{1}^{′}(t)} = L{-3x_{1}(t)}; L{x_{2}^{′}(t)} = L{9x_{1}(t) + 5x_{2}(t)}\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n{sL{x_{1}(t)} - x_{1}(0) =-3L{x_{1}(t)}; sL{x_{2}(t)} - x_{2}(0) = 9L{x_{1}(t)} + 5L{x_{2}(t)}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n{sX_{1}(s) - 3 =-3X_{1}(s); sX_{2}(s) - 7 = 9X_{1}(s) + 5X_{2}(s)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nX_1(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{x_1(t) \\right\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nX_2(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{x_2(t) \\right\\}\n$$\n\nare the Laplace transforms of the functions $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t)$, respectively."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167101 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c81626a0-6613-5f5d-9dda-df853958c571" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution of a Laplace Transformed Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "32855a04-54d2-565d-a5de-cad0c9bb1078" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163996}] :question/id #uuid "352d1492-cbac-52be-ab13-b4b1c7ef68ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-322291" :question/problem "${x_{1}^{′}(t) = x_{1}(t) - 2x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) =-x_{1}(t) + 2x_{2}(t),x(0) = \\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables $X_{1}(s) = L{x_{1}(t)}$ and $X_{2}(s) = L{x_{2}(t)}$. In terms of the parameter $s$, the solution of the new system is$X_{1}(s) =$ {{field-1}},\n$X_{2}(s) = (3(2s - 5))/(s(s - 3))$.\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"solving-an-initial-value-problem-given-the-laplace-transformed-solution\"></a>"}]}
